# Line-ending policy.
#
# Only the cases where the wrong ending is a real defect are pinned; everything
# else keeps git's default so this file doesn't churn the whole working tree.
#
# Shell scripts are the dangerous case. `install.sh` is fetched and piped
# straight into `sh` by the documented install command, and a CR at the end of
# each line makes a POSIX shell fail with `$'\r': command not found` — or, more
# insidiously, succeed while appending a CR to every variable it sets, so the
# download URL it builds is subtly wrong. `core.autocrlf=true` (the Git for
# Windows default) already normalizes these to LF on commit, but that depends on
# each contributor's local config; `eol=lf` makes it a property of the
# repository instead, and keeps the working tree LF too so Docker builds and
# shell-extracting tests (install-sh-*.test.ts) see what actually ships.
*.sh text eol=lf

# PowerShell is the mirror case: it is only ever run on Windows.
*.ps1 text eol=crlf

# Never let line-ending conversion touch a binary — a single byte rewritten
# inside a grammar or a native module corrupts it silently.
*.wasm binary
*.node binary
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.ico binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
